# direnv configuration. Run `direnv allow` once; after that these are exported
# whenever you cd into the repository.
#
# This file is committed and contains no values. Real ones go in `.envrc.local`,
# which is gitignored:
#
#     # .envrc.local
#     export SENTRY_DSN="https://…@….ingest.sentry.io/…"
#     export DEVELOPMENT_TEAM="XXXXXXXXXX"
#
# Both are OPTIONAL, and nothing in the day-to-day loop needs them:
#
#   ./dev            works — the app runs
#   ./dev test       works
#   ./dev lint       works
#   ./dev build      works — plain `cargo build`; DEVELOPMENT_TEAM only picks a
#                    codesign identity, and `codesign` falls back to an ad-hoc
#                    signature without it
#   ./dev publish    NOT IMPLEMENTED — refuses before minting a tag (#15)
#
# SENTRY_DSN is the crash reporter's destination, read at COMPILE time by
# `crates/crashreport` (#309). Two things gate it, and both have to hold:
#
#   1. The operator has ticked Settings → General → Crash Reporting. It is OFF
#      by default and the toggle is the only opt-in — a DSN being present is
#      not consent, and never becomes consent.
#   2. This build was compiled with a DSN.
#
# Without a DSN — the normal state of a clean clone, and of every build you
# compile yourself — the reporter is a silent no-op: nothing is sent, nothing
# errors, and the Settings tab says so out loud rather than implying otherwise.
# So there is still no reason for a contributor to set this.
#
# Wiring it into the release build is #307; `scripts/publish.sh` is #15's and
# still refuses before it reaches this value. `crates/usage/src/sentry.rs`
# remains unrelated to all of it: that one *reads* Sentry's API for the Usage
# and Crons panels, with an `org:read` token rather than a DSN.
#
# So a contributor can ignore this file entirely. It exists so that the one
# person cutting releases does not have to remember to export things by hand.
#
# If you pull these from a secret manager, do it here in `.envrc.local` — e.g.
#     eval "$(your-cli export --format shell)"
# No build script knows or cares where the values came from, which is the whole
# arrangement: a script that knows the source has to be edited every time the
# source changes, and every contributor has to learn the answer.

source_env_if_exists .envrc.local
